Here, $p_{i}^{\mu}$ and $\varepsilon_{i}^{\mu}\varepsilon_{i}^{\nu}$ are the momenta and polarization of the gravitons, and $v^{\mu}$ denotes the velocity of the compact object. The frequency of the external gravitons is defined by $\omega=p_{1}\cdot v=p_{2}\cdot v$ , and the momentum transferred in the process is $q^{\mu}=p_{2}^{\mu}-p_{1}^{\mu}$ . The magnitude of $q^{\mu}$ is related to the spatial angle $\theta$ between the two graviton momenta and is encoded in $x=\frac{|q|}{2\omega}=\sin(\frac{\theta}{2})\,,\ 0\leq x\leq 1\,.$ The Compton amplitude, which has a non-trivial functional dependence on $x$ , can be systematically expanded in a post-Minkowskian series, $n$ PM signifying the $n$ th order term, and in a series in the dimensional regulator $\epsilon$ so that

$$ \displaystyle\mathcal{M}=\mathcal{M}_{0}^{\rm 1PM}+\sum_{n=2}^{\infty}\sum_{j=1-n}^{\infty}{\epsilon^{j}}\mathcal{M}_{j}^{n\rm PM}, $$

where terms with $j<0$ represent infrared divergences. From Weinberg’s soft graviton theorem [110], the infrared divergences may be extracted by factoring out the Weinberg phase from the amplitude [121],

$$ \displaystyle\mathcal{M}_{\rm finite}(\mu,\varepsilon_{1},\varepsilon^{*}_{2})=e^{\mathrm{i}\bar{\epsilon}/\epsilon}\,\mathcal{M}\big|_{\epsilon\rightarrow 0}\, \displaystyle=\mathcal{M}_{0}^{\rm 1PM}+\mathcal{M}_{0}^{\rm 2PM}+\big(\mathcal{M}_{0}^{\rm 3PM}+\mathrm{i}\bar{\epsilon}\mathcal{M}_{1}^{\rm 2PM}\big)+\cdots, $$

where $\bar{\epsilon}=2GM\omega$ is the post-Minkowskian expansion parameter. Note at third post-Minkowskian order that $\mathcal{M}_{\rm finite}$ has a contribution from the $\epsilon$ -linear $\mathcal{M}_{1}^{\rm 2PM}$ 11 1 We thank Gustav Uhre Jakobsen for discussions on this point.. More generally, at higher orders in the post-Minkowskian expansion, higher powers of $\epsilon$ in the amplitude can also contribute to the finite amplitude and hence contribute to the physical differential cross section 22 2 We note that the generalized Compton amplitudes contribute to the cross section only at quantum level (see amplitude in [166] for QED).,

$$ \displaystyle{\mathrm{d}\sigma\over\mathrm{d}\Omega}={|\mathcal{M}_{\rm finite}(\mu,\varepsilon_{1},\varepsilon_{2}^{*})|^{2}\over(8\pi M)^{2}}\,. $$

Therefore, positive powers of $\epsilon$ in the amplitude cannot be discarded a priori. We also note that the $\mu$ -dependence in the amplitude cancels in the cross section.

All Feynman rules required for our computation are extracted from the action in eq. (1) by inserting the background field expansions,

$$ g_{\mu\nu}(x)=\bar{g}_{\mu\nu}(x)+\kappa_{d}h_{\mu\nu}(x),\quad x^{\mu}(\tau)=v^{\mu}\tau+z^{\mu}(\tau), $$

such that the metric is expanded around the Schwarzschild–Tangherlini solution [124] in isotropic coordinates, and the massive particle trajectory around straight-line motion [104, 107, 108]. Expanding around this solution resums certain classes of Feynman diagrams, reducing the number of diagrams one has to evaluate. To construct the integrand for the third-order Compton amplitude, we add the 13 contributing diagrams 33 3 We use xAct [167] to carry out the index contractions in the diagrams.,

