## III Comparison with black hole perturbation theory

In black hole perturbation theory, the helicity-conserving and helicity-reversing contributions to the differential cross section are expressed as an infinite sum involving spin-weighted spherical harmonic functions [146, 119, 147] written here in a modified form,

$$ \displaystyle f_{\rm B}(\theta) \displaystyle=\frac{\pi e^{-i\Phi}}{i\omega}\sum_{l=2}^{\infty}{}_{-2}Y_{l2}(0)\,{}_{-2}Y_{l2}(\theta)\,\Delta^{+}_{l2}, \displaystyle g_{\rm B}(\theta) \displaystyle=\frac{\pi e^{-i\Phi}}{i\omega}\sum_{l=2}^{\infty}(-1)^{l}{}_{-2}Y_{l2}(0)\,{}_{-2}Y_{l2}(\pi-\theta)\Delta^{-}_{l2}, $$

where $\Phi\equiv 2\bar{\epsilon}\ln(2\bar{\epsilon})-\bar{\epsilon}$ and $\Delta^{\pm}_{l2}=e^{2i\delta^{+}_{l2}}\pm e^{2i\delta^{-}_{l2}}$ . We drop the subtraction of $2$ in the original $f_{\rm B}(\theta)$ to make the definition consistent with the post-Minkowskian amplitude and also multiply by an $l$ -independent overall phase that does not affect the physical differential cross section. The parity- and $l$ -dependent phases are obtained from the Mano-Suzuki-Takasugi solution [148] of the Teukolsky equation [113, 114]

$$ \displaystyle e^{2i\delta_{lm}^{\pm}} \displaystyle=(-1)^{l+1}{C^{(0)}_{\rm TS}\over 16\omega^{4}}{B^{\rm(ref)}_{{lm}}\over B^{\rm(inc)}_{{lm}}}, $$

where $C^{(0)}_{\rm TS}=l(l^{2}-1)(l+2)\pm 6i\bar{\epsilon}$ is the Teukolsky-Starobinsky constant in the spinless limit. $B^{\rm(ref)}$ and $B^{\rm(inc)}$ are obtained from the asymptotic behavior of the Mano-Suzuki-Takasugi solution for the Newman-Penrose scalar $\psi_{4}$ and we refer to [148, 149] for their explicit values 55 5 We recommend the efficient program described in [168] for calculating all the relevant quantities and also point the reader to [169] for a program to calculate phase shifts using [168].. If we define components of the scattering amplitudes under projection to the spin-weighted spherical harmonic basis as

$$ \displaystyle(f)^{(l)} \displaystyle\equiv 2\pi\int_{0}^{\pi}f(\theta)\,{}_{-2}Y_{l2}(\theta)\sin(\theta)d\theta, \displaystyle(g)^{(l)} \displaystyle\equiv 2\pi\int_{0}^{\pi}g(\theta)\,{}_{-2}Y_{l2}(\pi-\theta)\sin(\theta)d\theta, $$

it is straightforward to deduce a non-perturbative relation between helicity-conserving and helicity-reversing functions,

$$ \displaystyle g^{(l)}_{\rm B}={6i\bar{\epsilon}\over(-1)^{l}l(l^{2}-1)(l+2)}f^{(l)}_{\rm B}, $$

as the parity-dependent phase arises only from the Teukolsky-Starobinsky constant. This relation is also proved in [151] based on Zerilli’s radial equation [112], see also [119].

To demonstrate the equivalence between the Compton amplitude in a post-Minkowskian expansion and expressions from black hole perturbation theory, it is useful to project the Compton amplitude onto the same complete basis of spherical harmonic functions. In order to do so and match known results, we set the scale parameter in the Compton amplitude to $\mu=2e^{-\gamma}\omega$ , and first proceed to regularize the forward scattering divergences in eq. (15) that prevent successful projection. Covariantizing the results of long-range (Newtonian) contributions to gravitational wave scattering in [152, 119], we find that a resummation of the leading forward divergent components from each post-Minkowskian order suffices as a natural regularization. While this contribution contains terms at all orders in the post-Minkowskian expansion, it arises solely from iterations of the tree-level Newtonian potential. Thus, we arrive at

$$ \displaystyle\mathcal{M}_{\rm finite}(2e^{-\gamma}\omega,\varepsilon_{1}, \displaystyle\varepsilon_{2}^{*})=32\pi GM^{2}\frac{\Gamma(1-i\bar{\epsilon})}{\Gamma(1+i\bar{\epsilon})}\frac{\mathsf{F}_{1}^{2}}{4x^{2(1-i\bar{\epsilon})}} \displaystyle+ \displaystyle\sum_{n=2}^{\infty}\mathcal{M}_{\rm finite,rm}^{n\rm PM}(2e^{-\gamma}\omega,\varepsilon_{1},\varepsilon_{2}^{*})\,, $$

where the subscript “rm” denotes remaining terms that are free of the leading forward divergent contribution. We find (to the order we work) that the leading forward divergent contribution can be understood from considering the resummation of graphs with a double cut at each loop,

$$ \bullet\bullet\bullet $$

The logic behind this argument starts from the one-loop amplitude result, where one arrives at contributions with the form $(\log(x)+\gamma){1\over x^{2}}$ . When adding a new rung, these one-loop terms reappear together with new higher-order loop terms. Since the total contribution from summing all loop orders will consist of all lower order contributions plus new ones at each order, it is possible to conjecture the all-loop result from the pattern of explicitly computed terms. We find terms giving rise to $\frac{e^{2i\gamma\bar{\epsilon}}}{x^{2-2i\bar{\epsilon}}}$ which is consistent with

$$ \frac{\Gamma(1-i\bar{\epsilon})}{\Gamma(1+i\bar{\epsilon})}=e^{2i\gamma\bar{\epsilon}}e^{i\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{2n+1}{\bar{\epsilon}}^{2n+1}\zeta(2n+1)}\, $$

in $32\pi GM^{2}\frac{\Gamma(1-i\bar{\epsilon})}{\Gamma(1+i\bar{\epsilon})}\frac{\mathsf{F}_{1}^{2}}{4x^{2(1-i\bar{\epsilon})}}$ in (23). (We note that exponential factors which involve terms such as $\zeta(3)$ and other transcendental constants begin to appear in the loop diagrams from the fourth post-Minkowskian order, which necessitates the appearance of $\zeta(j)$ in the phase factor.) A different value of $\mu$ will result in an extra phase upon resummation which does not affect physics but prevents a complete match when comparing with black hole perturbation theory.

In the lab frame, the scattering functions are

$$ \displaystyle f^{\rm nt}_{\rm amp}(\theta)+f^{\rm rm}_{\rm amp}(\theta) \displaystyle={{\cal M}_{\rm finite}(2e^{-\gamma}\omega,\varepsilon^{+}_{1},\varepsilon_{2}^{+*})\over 8\pi M}, \displaystyle g^{\rm nt}_{\rm amp}(\theta)+g^{\rm rm}_{\rm amp}(\theta) \displaystyle={{\cal M}_{\rm finite}(2e^{-\gamma}\omega,\varepsilon^{+}_{1},\varepsilon_{2}^{-*})\over 8\pi M}, $$

where $f^{\rm nt}_{\rm amp}(\theta)$ denotes the long-range Newtonian contribution from the first term in (23) and $f^{\rm rm}_{\rm amp}(\theta)$ gets contributions from the remaining terms. The same holds for the helicity-reversing $g(\theta)$ .

For the resummed Newtonian part, the integration necessary to project to the spherical harmonic basis is well-defined via analytic continuation in $\bar{\epsilon}$ . An example for $l=2$ is

$$ \displaystyle f^{\rm nt(2)}_{\rm amp}{=}{\sqrt{5\pi}\over\omega}\bigg[-i+\big(2\gamma-\tfrac{25}{12}\big)\bar{\epsilon}+i\Big(\tfrac{415}{144}-\tfrac{25}{6}\gamma+2\gamma^{2}\Big)\bar{\epsilon}^{2} \displaystyle\hskip 28.45274pt-\Big(\tfrac{2}{3}\zeta(3)-\tfrac{25}{6}\gamma^{2}+\tfrac{4}{3}\gamma^{3}+\tfrac{415}{72}\gamma-\tfrac{5845}{1728}\Big)\bar{\epsilon}^{3}\bigg]. $$

The partial wave projection leads to a post-Minkowskian order mixing effect which is clearly illustrated by this example. The $\zeta(3)$ term, expected in the plane wave amplitude at the fourth post-Minkowskian order, actually contributes at the third post-Minkowskian order in the spherical harmonic basis. This arises from the singular integration of one term in resummed Newtonian part of the amplitude in (23), $-\frac{8i\pi}{3\omega}\int_{0}^{1}dxx(_{-2}Y_{22}){\bar{\epsilon}^{4}\zeta(3)\over x^{2-2i\bar{\epsilon}}}=\frac{4\sqrt{5\pi}\bar{\epsilon}^{3}\zeta(3)}{3\left(\bar{\epsilon}^{2}-3i\bar{\epsilon}-2\right)\omega}$ . The agreement of this term with black hole perturbation theory at $\bar{\epsilon}^{3}$ -order thus implies that the corresponding term in the Newtonian part in (23) is correct at the fourth post-Minkowskian order. Similarly, the $\bar{\epsilon}^{0}$ term in the spherical harmonic component in (26) actually originates from the first post-Minkowskian amplitude, which explains why we drop the subtraction of $2$ in the definition of $f_{B}$ .

The remaining terms in eq. (23) exhibit softer divergences in the projection integral at each post-Minkowskian order and can therefore be integrated directly. As a highly nontrivial check, we compare the projected components explicitly up to $l=10$ to $\bar{\epsilon}^{3}$ -order and find exact agreement between the amplitude and black hole perturbation theory results,

$$ \displaystyle f^{{\rm rm}(l)}_{\rm amp}+f^{{\rm nt}(l)}_{\rm amp}=f^{(l)}_{\rm B}, \displaystyle g^{{\rm rm}(l)}_{\rm amp}+g^{{\rm nt}(l)}_{\rm amp}=g^{(l)}_{\rm B}\,. $$

The match for higher values of $l$ should be straightforward and we expect this equivalence to continue to hold at fourth post-Minkowskian order directly and at higher post-Minkowskian orders after including all the finite-size effects from either completing the effective action, or equivalently, the four-point tree-level Compton amplitude. In particular, the non-perturbative relations (22) appearing on the black hole perturbation theory side suggest the existence of highly non-trivial non-perturbative relations between the complete helicity-conserving Compton amplitude and helicity-reversing one.

## IV Conclusion and outlook

In this letter, we explicitly demonstrate that a formalism that systematically incorporates recoil effects in a non-spinning background extends to obtaining the gravitational Compton amplitude to third post-Minkowskian order. We also employ Weinberg’s soft theorem to factorize infrared singularities in the Compton amplitude and regulate the leading forward divergences of the long-range Newtonian contributions through a resummed expression. This framework allows us to establish an explicit mapping between perturbative scattering amplitudes in the post-Minkowskian expansion and non-perturbative expressions formulated in terms of spin-weighted spherical harmonics within the Mano-Suzuki-Takasugi solution of black hole perturbation theory. It seems to be worthwhile to formally prove the proposed deduced form for the term regulating the all-order resummed Newtonian contribution by explicitly working to higher orders in the post-Minkowskian expansion.

A natural extension of this work is to incorporate finite-size effects—such as spin, tidal responses, and absorption—into gravitational Compton amplitudes, particularly in light of current astrophysical observations. Because the resummed Newtonian contribution systematically handles post-Minkowskian order mixing, the bridge between formalisms established here indicates that black hole perturbation theory might constrain tree-level Compton amplitudes directly at arbitrary spin order in a way that would enable a bypassing of the conventional separation between near- and far-zone dynamics.

The direct observables in the ringdown stage are the quasi-normal modes [153, 154, 155, 156, 157], which are naturally obtained from computations within black hole perturbation theory. A central open question is how the discrete quasi-normal-mode frequencies and their associated amplitudes arise from post-Minkowskian scattering amplitudes. Our work suggests that such an extraction can be achieved by partially resumming plane-wave amplitudes and systematically mapping them onto partial-wave observables, thereby providing a new pathway to derive ringdown physics from perturbative scattering, which in turn could be used to characterize the black hole spectrum from an action principle. Moreover, generalizing the framework to higher-multiplicity amplitudes is of considerable interest, as it may provide access to nonlinear features of the quasi-normal-mode spectrum [158, 159, 160, 161, 162] and thus further deepen the connection between scattering amplitudes and black hole dynamics. We leave these and other questions to future research.

