Supplemental Material – From the Gravitational Compton Amplitude to Black Hole Perturbation Theory

In this supplemental material, we present the details of the Compton amplitude computation and derivation of the cross section. We then briefly introduce the method for obtaining the canonical basis and provide explicit expressions for all master integrals. Finally, we present the helicity-conserving and helicity-reversing amplitudes in the lab frame. We also include a detailed comparison between the post-Minkowskian expanded amplitude and the amplitude derived from black hole perturbation theory.

## Appendix A Results for the amplitude and cross section

From [104], the finite parts of the first and second order post-Minkowskian Compton amplitudes are

$$ \displaystyle\mathcal{M}_{0}^{\rm 1PM} \displaystyle=(32\pi G)M^{2}{\mathsf{F}_{1}^{2}\over 4x^{2}} \displaystyle\mathcal{M}_{0}^{\rm 2PM} \displaystyle=(32\pi G)^{2}M^{3}{\omega} \displaystyle\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\times\Bigg[\mathsf{F}_{1}^{2}\Bigg(\frac{i\log\left(\frac{4\omega^{2}}{\mu^{2}}\right)}{64\pi x^{2}}-\frac{(x(15x+28)+15)(x-1)^{2}}{1024x(x+1)^{2}(2x^{2}-1)} \displaystyle\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!-\frac{i(4x^{2}-7)}{192\pi(2x^{4}-3x^{2}+1)}-\frac{i(2x^{4}-2x^{2}+1)\log(x)}{32\pi x^{2}(x^{2}-1)^{2}(2x^{2}-1)}\Bigg) \displaystyle\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!\!+{\mathsf{F}_{2}^{2}\over 2x^{2}-1}\Bigg(\frac{-i(14x^{4}-19x^{2}+11)}{1536\pi x^{2}(x^{2}-1)^{3}}+\frac{15x^{2}+28x+15}{4096(x+1)^{4}x} \displaystyle\hskip 30.00005pt+\frac{i(2x^{4}-3x^{2}+2)\log(x)}{128\pi(x^{2}-1)^{4}}\Bigg)\Bigg]\,. $$

We note that the product $\mathsf{F}_{1}\mathsf{F}_{2}$ , used in [104], is not linearly independent due to the Gram determinant identity,

$$ \mathsf{F}_{1}\mathsf{F}_{2}=\frac{\mathsf{F}_{2}^{2}+4x^{2}(x^{2}-1)\mathsf{F}_{1}^{2}}{2(1-2x^{2})}. $$

Starting at the second post-Minkowskian order, the amplitude depends on the fiducial mass scale $\mu$ arising from loop integrals. However, the physical observable–the cross section–must be independent of $\mu$ . This can be seen explicitly by factoring out the $\mu$ -dependent factors by power counting of the $d$ -dimensional coupling in the cross section,

$$ \displaystyle\lvert\mathcal{M}\rvert^{2} \displaystyle=\left(\frac{d\sigma}{d\Omega}\right)_{\rm LO}+\left(\frac{\tilde{\mu}}{\omega}\right)^{2\epsilon}\left[\left(\frac{d\sigma}{d\Omega}\right)_{\rm NLO}\Big|_{\tilde{\mu}\to\omega}+\mathcal{O}(\epsilon)\right] \displaystyle+\left(\frac{\tilde{\mu}}{\omega}\right)^{4\epsilon}\left[\left(\frac{d\sigma}{d\Omega}\right)_{\rm NNLO}\Big|_{\tilde{\mu}\to\omega}+\mathcal{O}(\epsilon)\right]+\cdots\,, $$

where $\tilde{\mu}\to\omega$ means $\mu\to\sqrt{4\pi}e^{-\gamma/2}\omega$ according to the definition. Similarly, $|\mathcal{M}_{\rm finite}|^{2}$ is also independent of $\mu$ , and therefore it is reasonable to treat the scale parameter $\mu$ appearing in $\mathcal{M}_{\rm finite}$ as a free parameter.

We list the coefficient of the finite part of the amplitude at third post-Minkowskian order as

$$ \displaystyle d_{1} \displaystyle=\frac{8\mathsf{F}_{1}^{2}x^{4}\left(x^{2}-1\right)^{2}\left(8x^{8}-30x^{6}+48x^{4}-35x^{2}+10\right)-2\mathsf{F}_{2}^{2}\left(-55x^{2}+2\left(4x^{8}-21x^{6}+54x^{4}-77x^{2}+63\right)x^{4}+10\right)}{x^{8}\left(x^{2}-1\right)^{4}\left(2x^{2}-1\right)}, \displaystyle d_{2} \displaystyle=-\frac{\left(15x^{6}-7x^{4}-7x^{2}+15\right)\left(4\mathsf{F}_{1}^{2}\left(x^{2}-1\right)^{2}-\mathsf{F}_{2}^{2}\right)}{8x\left(x^{2}-1\right)^{4}\left(2x^{2}-1\right)}, \displaystyle d_{3} \displaystyle=\frac{4\mathsf{F}_{1}^{2}\left(x^{2}-1\right)^{2}\left(2x^{4}-2x^{2}+1\right)+\mathsf{F}_{2}^{2}\left(-2x^{4}+3x^{2}-2\right)x^{2}}{2x^{2}\left(x^{2}-1\right)^{4}\left(2x^{2}-1\right)},\hskip 28.45274ptd_{4}=\frac{\left(2x^{4}-3x^{2}+2\right)\left(4\mathsf{F}_{1}^{2}\left(x^{2}-1\right)^{2}-\mathsf{F}_{2}^{2}\right)}{2\left(x^{2}-1\right)^{4}\left(2x^{2}-1\right)}, \displaystyle d_{5} \displaystyle=\frac{\mathsf{F}_{2}^{2}\left(517x^{10}-1650x^{8}+2725x^{6}-2488x^{4}+1200x^{2}-240\right)-4\mathsf{F}_{1}^{2}x^{4}\left(x^{2}-1\right)^{2}\left(341x^{6}-730x^{4}+693x^{2}-240\right)}{24x^{6}\left(x^{2}-1\right)^{4}\left(2x^{2}-1\right)}, \displaystyle d_{6} \displaystyle=\frac{8\mathsf{F}_{1}^{2}x^{2}\left(x^{2}-1\right)^{2}\left(4x^{2}-7\right)+\mathsf{F}_{2}^{2}\left(14x^{4}-19x^{2}+11\right)}{6x^{2}\left(x^{2}-1\right)^{3}\left(2x^{2}-1\right)},\hskip 28.45274ptd_{7}=-\frac{(x(15x+28)+15)\left(4\mathsf{F}_{1}^{2}\left(x^{2}-1\right)^{2}-\mathsf{F}_{2}^{2}\right)}{16x(x+1)^{4}\left(2x^{2}-1\right)}, \displaystyle d_{\text{Re}} \displaystyle=\frac{\mathsf{F}_{2}^{2}\left(36x^{10}-941x^{8}+2275x^{6}-2636x^{4}+1530x^{2}-360\right)-4\mathsf{F}_{1}^{2}x^{4}\left(x^{2}-1\right)^{2}\left(36x^{6}-529x^{4}+757x^{2}-360\right)}{36x^{6}\left(x^{2}-1\right)^{3}\left(2x^{2}-1\right)}, \displaystyle d_{\text{Im}} \displaystyle=i\pi\Big[-\frac{(x-1)^{2}\left(4\mathsf{F}_{1}^{2}\left(x^{2}-1\right)^{2}\left(30x^{6}+433x^{5}+80x^{4}-1091x^{3}-560x^{2}+720x+480\right)x^{4}\right)}{24x^{7}\left(x^{2}-1\right)^{4}\left(2x^{2}-1\right)} \displaystyle- \displaystyle\frac{(x-1)^{2}\mathsf{F}_{2}^{2}\left(-30x^{10}-521x^{9}+32x^{8}+2103x^{7}+632x^{6}-3444x^{5}-1664x^{4}+2560x^{3}+1520x^{2}-720x-480\right)}{24x^{7}\left(x^{2}-1\right)^{4}\left(2x^{2}-1\right)}\Big]. $$

The coefficients that enter the next-to-next-to-leading-order cross section are

$$ \displaystyle c_{1} \displaystyle=16\left(x^{2}+\frac{2}{x^{2}}-3\right),~~~c_{2}=\frac{15x^{6}-7x^{4}-7x^{2}+15}{4x^{3}}, \displaystyle c_{3} \displaystyle=\frac{53-255x^{10}+1169x^{8}-2015x^{6}+1614x^{4}-548x^{2}}{18x^{2}(x^{2}-1)^{2}}, \displaystyle c_{4} \displaystyle=\frac{(x-1)^{4}\left(15x^{2}+28x+15\right)^{2}}{256x^{2}(x+1)^{4}}-\frac{2\left(2x^{4}-3x^{2}+2\right)}{3x^{2}}, \displaystyle c_{5} \displaystyle=\frac{1}{6x^{2}(x^{2}-1)^{3}}\Big(88x^{12}-547x^{10}+1319x^{8}-1630x^{6} \displaystyle+986x^{4}-211x^{2}-29\Big), \displaystyle c_{6} \displaystyle=\frac{4(x^{4}-x^{2}+1)^{2}(2x^{4}-3x^{2}+2)}{x^{2}(x^{2}-1)^{4}}\,. $$

