## Appendix C Boundary integral values

The gravitational Compton amplitude is regular in the backward-scattering limit ( $x\to 1$ ). The apparent pole at $x=1$ appearing in the reduced integrand is spurious, as it originates in the tensor and IBP reduction. One can show this by carrying out the integrand reduction with backward-scattering kinematics imposed from the outset. Doing this, one finds that the amplitude is finite, implying our claim that any intermediate divergences one may encounter as $x\to 1$ must be spurious.

By contrast, the forward singularity, proportional to $1/x^{2}={4\omega^{2}\over|q|^{2}}$ , is physical and originates from the universal long-range Newtonian gravitational potential $1/r$ . A stronger forward singularity would correspond to a long-range interaction incompatible with Einstein gravity. For example, a $1/x^{3}$ singularity would generate a logarithmic potential, while a $1/x^{4}$ singularity would correspond to a linearly growing potential. Such behaviors are absent in both Newtonian gravity and general relativity. Therefore, the forward behavior of the amplitude is uniquely fixed to be at most $1/x^{2}$ .

The forward and backward limits thus probe two independent physical properties of the amplitude: the universal long-range gravitational interaction and the regularity of the backward-scattering kinematics, respectively. Together they provide constraints on the boundary values,

$$ \displaystyle\mathcal{K}_{0,7}^{(0)} \displaystyle=-\frac{3}{4}\mathcal{K}_{0,1}^{(0)}-\mathcal{K}_{0,2}^{(0)},\quad\mathcal{K}_{0,8}^{(0)}=\frac{1}{4}\mathcal{K}_{0,1}^{(0)}, \displaystyle\mathcal{K}_{0,7}^{(1)} \displaystyle=\frac{1}{4}\left(-3\mathcal{K}_{0,1}^{(1)}-4\mathcal{K}_{0,2}^{(1)}+8\mathcal{K}_{0,5}^{(1)}\right),\quad\mathcal{K}_{0,8}^{(1)}=\frac{1}{4}\mathcal{K}_{0,1}^{(1)}, \displaystyle\mathcal{K}_{0,7}^{(2)} \displaystyle=-\frac{3}{4}\mathcal{K}_{0,1}^{(2)}+\pi^{2}\left(\mathcal{K}_{0,2}^{(0)}-\frac{1}{4}\mathcal{K}_{0,1}^{(0)}\right)-\mathcal{K}_{0,2}^{(2)}+2\mathcal{K}_{0,5}^{(2)} \displaystyle+\log(16)\mathcal{K}_{0,5}^{(1)}, \displaystyle\mathcal{K}_{0,8}^{(2)} \displaystyle=\frac{1}{12}\Big(3\mathcal{K}_{0,1}^{(2)}-\pi^{2}\left(\mathcal{K}_{0,1}^{(0)}-4\mathcal{K}_{0,2}^{(0)}\right) \displaystyle+8\mathcal{K}_{0,2}^{(2)}-4\mathcal{K}_{0,4}^{(2)}+8\mathcal{K}_{0,5}^{(2)}+16\log(2)\mathcal{K}_{0,5}^{(1)}\Big). $$

We denote the boundary values of the canonical master integrals by $\mathcal{K}_{0,j}^{(r)}$ , where $r=0,1,2$ labels the order in the $\epsilon$ expansion and $j$ labels the master integrals. One can see the boundary master integral values for the top topology can be constrained by those of lower topologies, the latter being easy to calculate directly. We present their values up to $\mathcal{O}(\epsilon^{2})$ ,

$$ \displaystyle\mathcal{K}_{0,1}^{(0)} \displaystyle=-\frac{1}{128\pi^{2}},\qquad\mathcal{K}_{0,2}^{(0)}=\mathcal{K}_{0,3}^{(0)}=\frac{1}{512\pi^{2}}, \displaystyle\mathcal{K}_{0,4}^{(0)} \displaystyle=\frac{1}{256\pi^{2}},\qquad\mathcal{K}_{0,5}^{(0)}=\mathcal{K}_{0,6}^{(0)}=0, \displaystyle\mathcal{K}_{0,1}^{(1)} \displaystyle=\frac{\gamma-i\pi-\log(\pi)}{64\pi^{2}},\quad\mathcal{K}_{0,2}^{(1)}=\mathcal{K}_{0,3}^{(1)}=\frac{-\gamma+i\pi+\log(\pi)}{256\pi^{2}}, \displaystyle\mathcal{K}_{0,4}^{(1)} \displaystyle=-\frac{\gamma-\log(\pi)}{128\pi^{2}},\qquad\mathcal{K}_{0,5}^{(1)}=\mathcal{K}_{0,6}^{(1)}=-\frac{i}{256\pi}, \displaystyle\mathcal{K}_{0,1}^{(2)} \displaystyle=\frac{\left(2i\gamma+\pi-2i\log(\pi)\right)\left(2i\gamma+3\pi-2i\log(\pi)\right)}{256\pi^{2}}, \displaystyle\mathcal{K}_{0,2}^{(2)} \displaystyle=\mathcal{K}_{0,3}^{(2)}=-\frac{\pi^{2}-12(-\gamma+i\pi+\log(\pi))^{2}}{3072\pi^{2}}, \displaystyle\mathcal{K}_{0,4}^{(2)} \displaystyle=-\frac{-12\gamma^{2}+7\pi^{2}-12\log^{2}(\pi)+24\gamma\log(\pi)}{1536\pi^{2}}, \displaystyle\mathcal{K}_{0,5}^{(2)} \displaystyle=\mathcal{K}_{0,6}^{(2)}=\frac{i\left(\gamma+\log\left(\frac{2}{\pi}\right)\right)}{128\pi}. $$

## Appendix D Boundary integration

In this appendix, we provide a discussion of the integration regions of the third-order post-Minkowskian integral family and evaluate the boundary constants necessary to determine the master integrals that are the particular solution of the system of differential equations. All the boundary constants have been confirmed to be correct by a direct numerical calculation performed with pySecDec [170], including the ones needed for the double box sector, which we do not compute but rather determine by requiring that all spurious poles cancel in the amplitude.

Being able to evaluate the asymptotic expansion of our integrals in the forward-scattering limit $x\to 0$ is of particular interest to us. This can be accomplished using the method of regions [171, 172, 173]. In principle, we only need this to determine the boundary constant for the box-triangles. But, as the regions are quite simple, we provide a discussion at the level of the integral family. Ignoring numerator factors as they are immaterial for the region analysis and integrating out the delta functions, a generic member of the third-order post-Minkowskian integral family for the Compton amplitude is

$$ \displaystyle K \displaystyle=\int_{\ell_{1},\ell_{2}}\frac{\hat{\delta}(\ell_{1}\cdot v)\hat{\delta}(\ell_{2}\cdot v)}{\ell_{1}^{2\nu_{3}}\ell_{2}^{2\nu_{4}}(2\omega xQ-\ell_{1}-\ell_{2})^{2\nu_{5}}} \displaystyle\quad\times\frac{1}{(p_{1}+\ell_{1})^{2\nu_{6}}(p_{1}+\ell_{1}+\ell_{2})^{2\nu_{7}}} $$

where the $\mathrm{i}0^{+}$ prescription has been suppressed since it is immaterial for this discussion, and where we have additionally exhibited all dependence on $x$ by defining

$$ Q=\frac{q}{2\omega x},\qquad Q^{2}=-1, $$

which uses $|q|=2\omega x$ . The method of regions instructs us to scale the components of the loop momenta with all possible powers of $x$ . Of course, the delta functions force the temporal components to vanish, $v\cdot\ell_{i}=0$ , so we scale the spatial components as

$$ \bm{\ell}_{i}\sim x^{p_{i}}, $$

and examine which scalings lead to non-scaleless integrals after Taylor expansion of the integrand. We find that two such scalings exist for the integral in (45):

$$ \displaystyle\bm{\ell}_{i} \displaystyle\sim x^{0} \displaystyle\text{(normal region)}, \displaystyle\bm{\ell}_{i} \displaystyle\sim x^{1} \displaystyle\text{(collinear region)}. $$

The leading contribution to the integral in eq. (45) in the normal region is

$$ \displaystyle K\big|_{\text{(nor)}} \displaystyle=\int_{\ell_{1},\ell_{2}}\frac{\hat{\delta}(\ell_{1}\cdot v)\hat{\delta}(\ell_{2}\cdot v)}{\ell_{1}^{2\nu_{3}}\ell_{2}^{2\nu_{4}}(\ell_{1}+\ell_{2})^{2\nu_{5}}} \displaystyle\quad\times{1\over(p_{1}+\ell_{1})^{2\nu_{6}}(p_{1}+\ell_{1}+\ell_{2})^{2\nu_{7}}} $$

and clearly scales as $x^{0}$ . The leading contribution to the integral in the collinear region is

$$ \displaystyle K\big|_{\text{(col)}} \displaystyle=\int_{\ell_{1},\ell_{2}}\frac{\hat{\delta}(\ell_{1}\cdot v)\hat{\delta}(\ell_{2}\cdot v)}{\ell_{1}^{2\nu_{3}}\ell_{2}^{2\nu_{4}}(2\omega Q-\ell_{1}-\ell_{2})^{2\nu_{5}}} \displaystyle\quad\times\frac{x^{2(3-\nu_{3}-\nu_{4}-\nu_{5})-\nu_{6}-\nu_{7}-4\epsilon}}{[2p_{1}\cdot\ell_{1}]^{\nu_{6}}[2p_{1}\cdot(\ell_{1}+\ell_{2})]^{\nu_{7}}} $$

Thus, as long as $2(\nu_{3}+\nu_{4}+\nu_{5})+\nu_{6}+\nu_{7}>6$ , the dominant contribution to the integral in the forward limit will come from the collinear region. We note that, in the region expansion, we have $p_{1}\cdot q=0+\mathcal{O}(x^{2})$ .

The double bubble and the active double triangles do not depend on $x$ . As such, no information about them is gained from the differential equation, and we must hence evaluate them directly. Fortunately, this is quite simple. As this is also the case for the potential double triangle, we evaluate it directly. The box-triangles are dominated by the leading contribution in the collinear region, cf. the preceding section. Therefore, we evaluate them in this region to obtain the boundary constants. As explained in the main text, we refrain from explicitly computing the boundary constants for the integrals in the top sector, instead taking advantage of the physical constraints that the amplitude must obey—namely, the cancellation of all spurious poles.

The double bubble factorizes,

$$ \displaystyle K_{1} \displaystyle=\tilde{\mu}^{4\epsilon}\int_{\ell_{1},\ell_{2}}\frac{\hat{\delta}(\ell_{1}\cdot v)\hat{\delta}(\ell_{2}\cdot v)}{[(p_{1}+\ell_{1})^{2}+\mathrm{i}0^{+}][(p_{1}+\ell_{1}+\ell_{2})^{2}+\mathrm{i}0^{+}]} \displaystyle=\tilde{\mu}^{4\epsilon}\bigg[\int_{\bm{\ell}}\frac{1}{\bm{\ell}^{2}-\omega^{2}-\mathrm{i}0^{+}}\bigg]^{2} \displaystyle=-\frac{e^{-2\mathrm{i}\pi\epsilon}\Gamma\big(\epsilon-\frac{1}{2}\big)^{2}\tilde{\mu}^{4\epsilon}\omega^{2-4\epsilon}}{(4\pi)^{3-2\epsilon}}, $$

Next is the potential double triangle,

$$ K_{4}=\tilde{\mu}^{4\epsilon}\int_{\ell_{1},\ell_{2}}\frac{\hat{\delta}(\ell_{1}\cdot v)\hat{\delta}(\ell_{2}\cdot v)}{\ell_{1}^{2}\ell_{2}^{2}(q-\ell_{1}-\ell_{2})^{2}}. $$

As this integral is a three-fold convolution in momentum space, it is a product in position space. Hence, by expanding each propagator in plane waves using

$$ \frac{1}{\bm{\ell}^{2}}=\frac{2^{d-3}\Gamma\big(\frac{d-3}{2}\big)}{(4\pi)^{\frac{d-1}{2}}}\int\mathrm{d}^{d-1}\mathbf{x}\,\frac{\mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i}\bm{\ell}\cdot\mathbf{x}}}{|\mathbf{x}|^{d-3}}, $$

we obtain the representation

$$ K_{4}=\tilde{\mu}^{4\epsilon}\frac{8^{d-3}\Gamma\big(\frac{d-3}{2}\big)^{3}}{(4\pi)^{\frac{3(d-1)}{2}}}\int\mathrm{d}^{d-1}\mathbf{x}\,\frac{\mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i}\mathbf{q}\cdot\mathbf{x}}}{|\mathbf{x}|^{3(d-3)}}, $$

which is a standard Fourier integral leading to

$$ \displaystyle K_{4} \displaystyle=\frac{\Gamma\big(\frac{1}{2}-\epsilon\big)^{3}\Gamma(2\epsilon)}{(4\pi)^{3-2\epsilon}\Gamma\big(\frac{3(1-2\epsilon)}{2}\big)}\bigg(\frac{\tilde{\mu}^{2}}{4\omega^{2}x^{2}}\bigg)^{2\epsilon}. $$

The last integrals we must evaluate exactly are the active double triangles,

$$ \displaystyle K_{2} \displaystyle=\tilde{\mu}^{4\epsilon}\int_{\ell_{1},\ell_{2}}\frac{\hat{\delta}(\ell_{1}\cdot v)\hat{\delta}(\ell_{2}\cdot v)}{\ell_{1}^{2}\ell_{2}^{2}[(p_{1}+\ell_{1}+\ell_{2})^{2}+\mathrm{i}0^{+}]}, \displaystyle K_{3} \displaystyle=\tilde{\mu}^{4\epsilon}\int_{\ell_{1},\ell_{2}}\frac{\hat{\delta}(\ell_{1}\cdot v)\hat{\delta}(\ell_{2}\cdot v)}{\ell_{2}^{2}(q-\ell_{1}-\ell_{2})^{2}[(p_{1}+\ell_{1})^{2}+\mathrm{i}0^{+}]}. $$

They are equal, so we only evaluate $K_{1,1,0,1,1,1,0}^{0,0,0,0}$ . This can be done loop-by-loop, employing the standard formulae,

$$ \displaystyle\int_{\ell}\frac{\hat{\delta}(\ell\cdot v)}{\ell^{2}(k-\ell)^{2}}=\frac{\Gamma\big(\frac{1}{2}-\epsilon\big)^{2}\Gamma\big(\frac{1}{2}+\epsilon\big)}{(4\pi)^{\frac{3}{2}-\epsilon}\Gamma(1-2\epsilon)}\frac{1}{(-k^{2})^{\frac{1}{2}+\epsilon}}, \displaystyle\int_{\ell}\frac{\hat{\delta}(\ell\cdot v)}{(q-\ell)^{2\alpha}[(p_{1}+\ell)^{2}+\mathrm{i}0^{+}]} \displaystyle=\frac{\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\pi(2\alpha-\epsilon)}\Gamma(2-2\alpha-2\epsilon)\Gamma\big(\alpha+\epsilon-\frac{1}{2}\big)\omega^{1-2\alpha-2\epsilon}}{(4\pi)^{\frac{3}{2}-\epsilon}\Gamma(2-\alpha-2\epsilon)}, $$

where the first formula is valid for $k\cdot v=0$ and the second makes use of the on-shell identity $p_{1}\cdot q=-q^{2}/2$ . We obtain

$$ \displaystyle K_{3} \displaystyle=-\frac{\mathrm{e}^{2\mathrm{i}\pi\epsilon}\Gamma(2\epsilon)\Gamma\big(\frac{1}{2}-2\epsilon\big)\Gamma\big(\frac{1}{2}-\epsilon\big){\rm sec}\pi\epsilon}{64\,\Gamma\big(\frac{3}{2}-3\epsilon\big)}. $$

The two box-triangles,

$$ \displaystyle K_{5} \displaystyle=\tilde{\mu}^{4\epsilon}\int_{\ell_{1},\ell_{2}}\frac{\hat{\delta}(\ell_{1}\cdot v)\hat{\delta}(\ell_{2}\cdot v)}{\ell_{1}^{2}\ell_{2}^{2}(q-\ell_{1}-\ell_{2})^{2}(p_{1}+\ell_{1}+\ell_{2})^{2}}, \displaystyle K_{6} \displaystyle=\tilde{\mu}^{4\epsilon}\int_{\ell_{1},\ell_{2}}\frac{\hat{\delta}(\ell_{1}\cdot v)\hat{\delta}(\ell_{2}\cdot v)}{\ell_{1}^{2}\ell_{2}^{2}(q-\ell_{1}-\ell_{2})^{2}(p_{1}+\ell_{1})^{2}}, $$

are equal, just like the two double triangles. We evaluate the leading contribution to $K_{6}$ in the collinear region (see eq. (50)),

$$ K_{6}^{0,0,0,0}\big|_{\text{(col)}}=\int_{\ell_{1},\ell_{2}}\frac{x^{-1-4\epsilon}\hat{\delta}(\ell_{1}\cdot v)\hat{\delta}(\ell_{2}\cdot v)}{\ell_{1}^{2}\ell_{2}^{2}(2\omega Q-\ell_{1}-\ell_{2})^{2}(2p_{1}\cdot\ell_{1}+\mathrm{i}0^{+})}. $$

One now first integrates out $\ell_{2}$ using eq. (57a), after which one can finish the job by using the formula

$$ \displaystyle\int_{\ell}\frac{\hat{\delta}(\ell\cdot v)}{\ell^{2}(q-\ell)^{2\alpha}[p_{1}\cdot\ell+\mathrm{i}0^{+}]} \displaystyle=\frac{\mathrm{i}\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\pi\alpha}(4\pi)^{\epsilon}\Gamma(-\epsilon)\csc\big(\pi(\alpha+\epsilon)\big)}{8\Gamma(\alpha)\Gamma(1-\alpha-2\epsilon)(-q^{2})^{\alpha+\epsilon}\omega}\qquad\text{($p_{1}\cdot q=0$)}, $$

which can be derived by introducing Feynman parameters. This procedure yields the result

$$ \displaystyle K_{6}^{0,0,0,0}\big|_{\text{(col)}} \displaystyle=-\frac{\mathrm{i}\Gamma\big(\frac{1}{2}-\epsilon\big){\rm sec}{2\pi\epsilon}\,\tilde{\mu}^{4\epsilon}}{128(2\pi)^{1-2\epsilon}\epsilon\Gamma\big(\frac{1}{2}-3\epsilon\big)x^{1+4\epsilon}\omega^{2+4\epsilon}}, $$

from which one readily determines the requisite boundary constant.

